+          "section": "4",
+          "text": "# 4. Governance Principles\n\nThe six original principle headings remain. Apply them to the declared subject/domain and governing rules; they do not allocate new framework AP identifiers or silently override common Architecture/Policy or other semantic owners.\n\n---\n\n## 4.1. Permanence\n\nPreserve the assigned key's association with the same logical document under its governing domain contract. Retirement ends active use within its stated scope without releasing the allocated key for another document. Preserve historical association, reservations and intended reference interpretation; permanence does not guarantee live content or universally valid connections.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2. Immutability\n\nImmutability concerns the assigned Domain+Identifier key and same-document association, not every state/reference/descriptor/registration/projection record. Ordinary content, metadata, classification, document-status, edition, repository and format changes preserve that key while their own records may evolve under responsible owners.\n\nIf a necessary authorized migration would alter an otherwise immutable representation/allocation contract, first reconcile prior/new owner rules, actual authority, affected exact sources/consumers and dependencies, old→new mappings, aliases/reservations/collisions/history, target and exact-snapshot continuity, compatibility and transition checks. Never reassign an allocated/retired key to another document or retarget immutable historical Baseline members. A governance/validation label does not authorize mutation; this UPDATE executes no migration.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3. Uniqueness\n\nAn applicable assigned normalized logical document has one governing identity under its declared document/domain contract. Pre-allocation subjects, excluded functional files, other entity kinds and exact editions are not additional required DF document keys.\n\nUniqueness concerns full qualified Domain+Identifier, actual canonical/alias interpretation and scoped assignment/population/history. Multiple copies, repeated registrations, graph projections or editions about one logical key are not by themselves duplicate allocations. Actual competing document assignments or incompatible canonical interpretations need observed evidence under the responsible rules.\n\nFor this corpus, the domain is Documentation Framework, represented by DF. DF-SSS-NNN uses a registered uppercase three-letter stable allocation series and positive three-digit001–999 allocation. Current Family/Type/Component/path/edition/status do not regenerate the key; equal numeric suffixes across different full series identifiers do not alone collide. 000 is excluded; gaps prove no availability; allocated/retired numbers remain reserved. At999, register a traceable unused continuation series rather than widen or recycle silently. DID-005§4.2 and Policy§4 govern detailed corpus rules; external domains need their own explicit contracts.\n\nRecord actual domain/series authority, collision/reservation/current-historical population checks and coverage. Syntax success or registration alone proves no unique allocation or global resolver.\n\n---\n\n## 4.4. Non-Reusability\n\nAllocated and retired keys remain reserved against assignment to a different logical document. Actual retirement scope/decision, reservation history and same-document association must remain traceable. Retirement neither erases prior identity evidence nor guarantees all indexes were updated or content remains retrievable.\n\nA gap, missing record or absent live content does not establish that a number is available. Distinguish observed reassignment from missing history; retain actual violations and evidence rather than rewriting the past. This source allocates, retires and reassigns no key.\n\n---\n\n## 4.5. Traceability\n\nPreserve assignment, evolution, retirement, rule/decision and reference provenance for the intended qualified document or exact edition/context. Long-term historical interpretation is a requirement; actual target validity, freshness and current/future live retrieval remain separately observed facts.\n\nA valid document-targeting reference identifies its intended existing qualified logical document or exact edition with the additional coordinates/bytes and context needed. Other endpoints follow their own contracts; optional incoming0..N does not require a reference merely for a key to exist. Keep dangling, ambiguous, stale and unavailable observations explicit. Never silently create or retarget a key to make a reference appear valid, and never retarget immutable historical snapshot members.\n\n---\n\n## 4.6. Independence\n\nThe six original independence subjects remain: document content, metadata, governance workflows, repositories, implementation technologies and storage mechanisms. Ordinary changes there do not automatically redefine or reallocate identity. This is semantic separation, not absence of actual evidence, interactions, owner requirements or operational decisions.\n\nIdentity governance may inspect relevant content, descriptors, representations, state/decision records, registrations and references without taking over their complete validation or semantics. Logical representation/interface and adapter requirements can be legitimate while storage/runtime choices remain open. Required contracts, including actual reciprocal Metadata/Identity claims, must not be hidden by an independence or projection label.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "5",
+          "text": "# 5. Governance Responsibilities\n\nThe six original identity-governance responsibility subjects remain with bounded meaning.\n\n| Responsibility | Actual contract responsibility / evidence boundary |\n|---|---|\n| Architectural definition of identity | Document Identity owns document identity meaning under applicable common architecture and domain rules |\n| Identity ownership | Preserve semantic owner versus other descriptor/connection/registration/projection owners |\n| Identity integrity | State stable assigned key/association and actual record/transition evidence requirements |\n| Identity permanence | Define history/reservation/non-reuse and intended target-continuity requirements |\n| Identity uniqueness | Define qualified-domain/key and actual population/collision/allocation coverage requirements |\n| Identity consistency | Define applicable rule/subject/context assessment and retain unresolved owner/consumer conflicts |\n\nThe seven original operational exclusions are preserved:\n\n- document approval;\n- document reviews;\n- document publication;\n- document lifecycle management;\n- repository administration;\n- access control;\n- workflow execution.\n\nDocument Identity does not take over these operations by defining identity-governance rules. Workflow/Policies/Reviews/Baselines and applicable other domain owners define their contracts; actual DMS/tool/manual execution and CMS change governance remain separate. Identity lifecycle assertions are not the entire document lifecycle management operation. A review or validation can use identity evidence without transferring identity meaning or granting approval.\n\nFor a real identity-affecting decision, record the responsible applicable authority/rules, exact subject/domain/artifacts/editions/context, proposed action, actual executor, observations, affected owners/consumers and limits. No named user, permission, organizational role assignment or implemented process is fabricated by these evidence roles. Cumulative documentary Author and event-specific contribution are provenance, not automatic decision authority.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "8",
+          "text": "# 8. Governance Interactions\n\nThe six original interaction headings remain. Common documentary authority, semantic ownership, required consumption, evidence inspection, actual decisions, operational execution and data projection are distinct roles with explicit sources/editions/context.\n\n---\n\n## 8.1. Metadata\n\nMetadata represents identity/state/edition facts under their responsible semantic owners; its own descriptors/schema/profile/instance definitions remain separately governed. Ordinary descriptor changes do not reallocate assigned identity. Inspecting those values for identity decisions/checks does not establish complete Metadata validation or create a missing canonical Metadata Element/Profile.\n\nThe functional DID README declares identity consumption of Metadata descriptive concepts, while DID-009§§5/10 declares Metadata→Document Identity/no consuming-component dependency. Preserve both actual required-contract claims for owner/core-versus-descriptor/interface reconciliation under ARC-004. Different names or projected values do not prove independence or resolve a genuine required cycle.\n\n---\n\n## 8.2. Classification\n\nClassification governs category meanings. Ordinary Family/Type/Component changes preserve the stable allocated key/series and do not regenerate identity. Actual category/entity/edition effects still need their applicable owner rules and evidence; category-definition keys are not automatically normalized document IDs. Identity governance does not validate the whole Classification component by inspecting labels.\n\n---\n\n## 8.3. Governance Layer\n\nThe received heading Governance Layer denotes the Governance structural area: Workflow, Policies, Reviews and Baselines define documentary transition, decision, review and exact-snapshot contracts. It is not a single invented component or an operational process engine.\n\nActual operation belongs to the applicable DMS/tool/manual executor; CMS governs changes in its scope. Record responsible authority/rules, intended exact subject/edition/context, actual decision/executor and observations. Do not infer user permissions or implemented workflows from an architectural role description.\n\nIdentity-domain governance preserves its assigned-key/record requirements while those owners retain document approval/review/publication/lifecycle/process/snapshot responsibilities. Validation, approval, publication, document Status, identity lifecycle, maturity and actual Baseline inclusion are distinct. Logical identity or a local PASS cannot select an exact approved member, approve a Draft successor or retarget an immutable historical snapshot.\n\n---\n\n## 8.4. Registry\n\nRegistry governs registration/index records under source/edition provenance, scope and freshness/current-historical-snapshot context. Their values can change while retaining the intended qualified key. Registration alone is not allocation, proof of uniqueness, completed retirement propagation or actual governance approval. Identity definition remains with its semantic owner; no universal Registry intermediary or live resolver is imposed.\n\n---\n\n## 8.5. Relationships\n\nRelationships owns typed connection semantics/records and provenance. Document Identity supplies document endpoint meaning; other endpoint kinds follow their own domains. Governed connection-record evolution does not automatically change assigned identity.\n\nActual target/canonicalization/exact-edition/context validity and live retrieval need observations. Preserve intended historical interpretation, expose dangling/ambiguous/stale/unavailable references and never silently retarget keys or exact snapshot members. Optional incoming0..N is separate from valid per-reference targeting.\n\n---\n\n## 8.6. Knowledge Graph\n\nKnowledge Graph owns derived graph representation/projection responsibilities under underlying identity/relationship authority. Logical document keys can remain stable while node properties, edges, state descriptions, physical representation and freshness change. A projection creates neither competing identity semantics nor proof of full current consistency.\n\nActual required consumer→provider use, rule/source interfaces and cycle assessment remain explicit under ARC-004/005. Identity governance neither dictates a universal Registry→Graph→Baseline chain nor certifies a complete globally cycle-free or implemented graph.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "9",
+          "text": "# 9. Governance Compliance\n\nThe six original compliance conditions remain with explicit scope and evidence. They define requirements for an identified claim, not unconditional architectural certification from labels.\n\n| Received compliance condition | Qualified requirement and actual evidence boundary |\n|---|---|\n| Identifier is unique | Full qualified domain/key/canonical interpretation over declared actual population/history, with collision/reservation limits |\n| Identity remains immutable | Assigned same-document key under governing contract, not every mutable state/reference/descriptor record |\n| Canonical representation is preserved | Actual responsible representation/alias and prior/current artifact/context checks; any necessary contract transition separately assessed |\n| References remain valid | Actual typed intended target/edition/context and observed resolution; historical interpretation differs from live retrieval and no perpetual validity is promised |\n| Architectural constraints are satisfied | Identify applicable exact owner clauses, subject/scope, actual observations and unresolved authority/coverage |\n| Validation rules are fulfilled | Actual performed applicable checks and evidence under DID-007 result/coverage semantics; source presence or successful subset is insufficient |\n\nA compliance claim needs exact subject/domain/artifacts/editions/context, responsible rule set, executor/method, performed checks/observations, omissions, evidence/authority limits and actual claim coverage. DID-007§8 separates actual Valid/Invalid/Inconsistent/Unresolvable observations from missing/unexecuted evidence or unresolved owner authority. A partial successful check is not full identity, peer or framework conformity; no automatic approval, publication or Baseline follows.\n\nMandatory State/Canonical Reference and complete-record/lifecycle/constraint authority in DID-005/006/014 remain actual owner work. Do not waive requirements, invent a merged record or select a conflicting rule merely to claim success. Unambiguous narrower observations may be reported with their limits; the broader claim remains withheld where applicable evidence or authority is unresolved.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "10",
+          "text": "# 10. Governance Violations\n\nThe six original violation subjects remain, now tied to actual governing rule/subject/context and observed evidence. A condition is an actual violation only when the applicable requirement and observed nonconformance are established; suspected problems, missing history or unresolved owner authority are recorded with those limits rather than fabricated into failures or success.\n\n| Received violation subject | Actual violating meaning / legitimate distinction |\n|---|---|\n| Modifying an assigned identity | Unauthorized or nonconforming change of the governed key/same-document association; ordinary mutable support records are not all key modifications, and a necessary contract migration requires prior/new reconciliation before application |\n| Assigning multiple identities to the same document | Competing allocations contrary to the applicable document/domain/canonical contract; exact editions, aliases or representations are not automatically different logical identities |\n| Assigning the same identity to multiple documents | Observed conflicting assignment of a full qualified key to different logical documents in the governed domain; equal numeric suffixes across different series or copies of one document are not alone a violation |\n| Reusing a retired identity | Actual reassignment of a reserved key to a different document; missing records or live content do not establish availability |\n| Creating implementation-specific identity semantics | Changing responsible logical meaning through implementation; legitimate domain-preserving adapters/representations and applicable interface constraints are not automatically violations |\n| Bypassing canonical identity definitions | Actual circumvention of governing responsible rules; common Architecture/Policy and other domain owners remain applicable, and actual peer authority conflicts must be reconciled rather than ignored |\n\nFor an observed or suspected issue:\n\n1. Preserve exact original artifacts/records, applicable rules/editions, intended subject/domain/context, observations and coverage; state whether a violation is established or evidence/authority is missing.\n2. Identify responsible semantic owners and actual decision/execution authority for the proposed correction. A review finding, governance label or validation result alone grants no mutation, allocation, operational permission, approval or publication.\n3. Before a necessary normative change or identity/path/representation migration, assess prior/new contract, rationale, affected owners/consumers and actual required dependencies; record compatibility, mappings, reservations/history/aliases/reference and exact-snapshot continuity plus transition checks.\n4. Execute only the separately authorized scoped change with actual contributor/event provenance; preserve historical failed checks and original evidence. No automatic repair or silent target/identity creation is performed by governance evaluation.\n5. Validate the exact changed artifacts/editions in the appropriate subsequent operation and report coverage/remaining conflicts. Do not rewrite an earlier failure or pending event as if it had always passed.\n\nFor changed working documents, compatible correction retains Version; compatible additive capability increments minor; incompatible contract change increments major and resets minor to0. Revision increments monotonically for each persisted changed edition, including across Version changes. Validation with unchanged source changes neither coordinate and is recorded centrally under Policy§5. Other entity allocation/lifecycle remains separate. No source ID migration, allocation, actual correction operation or global remediation is executed by this procedure description.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "13",
+          "text": "# 13. Architectural Constraints\n\nThe six original independence constraints remain with qualified semantic and operational boundaries.\n\n| Received constraint | Qualified requirement / evidence boundary |\n|---|---|\n| Technology independent | Identity meaning is not defined by a platform; applicable logical representation/interface/adapter requirements and actual evidence can still matter |\n| Implementation independent | Domain ownership differs from actual executing systems; no implementation may silently redefine semantics, but legitimate representations are permitted under governing rules |\n| Repository independent | Ordinary moves preserve assigned identity; actual location/index/reference/context changes still need evidence |\n| Storage independent | Storage format does not allocate a new logical key by itself; persistence/representation/provenance requirements remain applicable |\n| Workflow independent | Key meaning is not a workflow status, while responsible process/decision contracts and actual operations can require coordinated evidence |\n| Organisation independent | Conceptual domain contract does not invent an organization's people/permissions; actual authority/executor decisions must still be evidenced for real operations |\n\nNo implementation technology is prescribed here. That does not prohibit legitimate logical format/canonicalization/interface constraints or evidence about actual adapters/executors. Independence does not prove implemented interoperability, fully separate required dependencies or universal future outcomes. Record exact rules/artifacts/context, checks, executor, observations, coverage and limits.\n\n---\n\n"
+        }
